[Event "Knight School"]
[Opening "Sicilian English Attack"]
[Variation "Sicilian Defense: Najdorf Variation, English Attack"]
[ECO "B90"]
[Character "sharp,tactical"]
[Risk "medium"]
[EarlyMate "none"]
[Tricks "none"]
[Themes "opposite-castling,pawn-storm"]

1. e4 { Takes space in the centre and opens lines for the queen and the f1 bishop. } 1... c5
2. Nf3 { Develops a piece and prepares d4, which is the whole idea of the Open Sicilian. } 2... d6
3. d4 { Offers a pawn trade that gives White the lead in the centre and development. } 3... cxd4
4. Nxd4 { Recaptures with a piece that now sits in the middle and eyes b5, f5 and e6. } 4... Nf6
5. Nc3 { Develops and defends the e4 pawn that the black knight attacks. } 5... a6
6. Be3 { Puts the bishop on a good diagonal that guards d4 and prepares f3, Qd2 and long castling. This is the start of the English Attack. [%csl Gd4] }
6... e5
7. Nb3 { The knight must leave d4. On b3 it is safe and still guards the queenside. }
7... Be6
(7... Be7 8. f3 { This reaches the main line by a different move order. } 8... Be6)
8. f3 { Guards the e4 pawn, so Black has no pressure on it, and prepares the g-pawn advance. }
8... Be7
(8... Nbd7 9. Qd2 Be7 10. g4)
9. Qd2 { Brings the queen out, connects with the e3 bishop and prepares castling on the queenside. }
9... O-O
10. O-O-O { White castles on the opposite side to Black. Now the pawns can march at Black's king without White's own king being in the way. }
10... Nbd7
11. g4 { Starts the pawn storm. The g-pawn is heading for g5 to harass the knight on f6. [%cal Gg4g5] }
11... b5
(11... Rc8 12. Kb1 { A useful king move off the c-file before Black can attack there. })
12. g5 { Hits the knight on f6. If it leaves, White's pawns and pieces roll towards the king. }
12... b4 *
